From 746bca1b4148885f05a65bcf52a2909b210ae25e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ren=C3=A9=20Rebe?= <rene@exactco.de>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 19:43:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] m68k: uaccess: Copy bytewise after a fault and unroll the
 copy loops
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

__generic_copy_from_user() reports nothing copied when the first word or
longword of a buffer crosses into an unmapped page, although the bytes
before the boundary are accessible.  generic_perform_write() then faults
the buffer in, finds it partially readable and retries the same copy
forever, so a two byte write() from a buffer one byte before an unmapped
page never returns.  The usercopy functions must make progress whenever
the first byte is accessible.

After a fault, retry bytewise from the first byte not copied.  The kernel
side pointer only advances after a successful user access, so it tells
where to restart.  __clear_user() has no kernel pointer and restarts
from the start of the current block instead, zeroing some bytes twice.
Instructions behind a store keep their exception table entries for the
68040 and 68060, which may report a store fault on the next instruction.

Copy and clear 32 bytes per loop iteration instead of four.  On MAME's
X68030 this speeds up 4 MB through a pipe by 26%, reading from the page
cache by 16% and writing to tmpfs by 16%, one byte system calls are
0.8% slower.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: René Rebe <rene@exactco.de>
---
